From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 03:14:55 +0200
Subject: net: lantiq: tighten the WAN port plumbing after review

Three defects found reviewing the dp-port-15 series, all in code it added.

The egress resolver admitted more datapath ports than the driver can
serve. Transcribing every Ethernet DQM-DMA row of the vendor's
cbm_config.c meant dp 1 and dp 6 resolved to dequeue ports 6 and 11 -
real GSWIP-L ports, but ones no board here wires and for which
hdma_port_chan_tbl() has no channel. Since that table is also the
admissibility test now, it silently widened three gates: a DT could
declare intel,dp-port-id = <1> and get a netdev that fails at ifup, and
the CBM probe would program DQM and TMU egress state for a port the board
does not have. Trim it to the rows served end to end, and say in the
comment which vendor rows are deliberately absent and why. The high-prio
twin rows (deq 12..17) go too - first-match keeps the primaries, so they
were unreachable data. The admissible set is now exactly {2,3,4,5,15}.

The lantiq,wan cross-check was one-directional. It rejected lantiq,wan on
a port other than 15 but accepted dp port 15 without lantiq,wan, which is
the quieter half of the same mistake: the port comes up, takes the LAN
PMAC template, and egresses with redirect = 0 - precisely the silent
class/template disagreement the check exists to catch. The two are
equivalent, so check the equivalence.

phy_register_fixup_for_uid was called once per bus. phylib's fixup list
is global and matches on PHY uid, so one registration already covers
every bus including the WAN PHY; a second makes the whole fixup run twice
per attach, and it starts with genphy_soft_reset() - the second pass would
reset the PHY again after the first had configured it, leaving correctness
resting on the two passes staying idempotent. Register once.

Also corrected four comments the series had left contradicting the code:
the 7590 DTS header still said the WAN half was absent, intel_xrx500.c's
header still gave dp_port_id + 5 as the rule, the dp_alloc_flags kerneldoc
still said LAN-always, and a new comment cited datapath_mib.c as if it
were in this tree rather than the vendor's.

Re-ran the extract-and-compile LAN-invariance proof against the trimmed
table: dp 2/3/4/5 still resolve bit-identically to the closed forms, dp 15
still gives deq 19 / queue 28 / sbid 12 / DMA1TX ch 15, and dp 1 and 6 are
now rejected. dtc confirms all three DTBs are unchanged by this commit.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/cqm/grx500/cbm_ports.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/cqm/grx500/cbm_ports.c
index 077f83e1bc7c..590a2da420d7 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/cqm/grx500/cbm_ports.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/cqm/grx500/cbm_ports.c
@@ -321,10 +321,10 @@ static u32 get_matching_EP(
 }
 
 /*
- * DQM-DMA rows of AVM's xrx500_cbm_config[] (cqm/grx500/cbm_config.c),
- * Ethernet subset: CBM dequeue port -> TMU queue + the DMA controller and
- * channel that drains it. num_desc is 2 on every row and lives in hdma.c as
- * CBM_DQM_DESC_NUM; tmu_queue_nos is 1 on every row here.
+ * DQM-DMA rows of AVM's xrx500_cbm_config[] (cqm/grx500/cbm_config.c): CBM
+ * dequeue port -> TMU queue + the DMA controller and channel that drains it.
+ * num_desc is 2 on every row and lives in hdma.c as CBM_DQM_DESC_NUM;
+ * tmu_queue_nos is 1 on every row here.
  *
  * dma_ctrl keeps AVM's own encoding (1 = DMA1TX, 2 = DMA2TX), not the cid
  * values of enum dma_controller — this table is a transcription of the vendor
@@ -332,6 +332,21 @@ static u32 get_matching_EP(
  *
  * The dp-15 row is the one that matters: dequeue 19 is drained by DMA1TX,
  * a different controller from every LAN port, not merely a different channel.
+ *
+ * Only the rows this driver can serve end to end are carried, because this
+ * table IS the admissibility test - it is what decides which intel,dp-port-id
+ * a board may declare, and which ports the CBM probe programs DQM and TMU
+ * state for. The vendor's remaining DQM-DMA rows are deliberately absent:
+ *
+ * deq 6 and 11 (dp 1 and 6) are real GSWIP-L ports, but no board here wires
+ * them and hdma_port_chan_tbl() has no channel for either, so admitting them
+ * would hand out a netdev that fails at ifup and program egress state at
+ * probe for a port that does not exist on the board. - deq 12..17 are the
+ * high-priority twins of the six LAN ports (AVM's intel,highprio-lan). They
+ * carry the same pmac values as the primaries, which take precedence in the
+ * lookup below, so these rows could never be selected even if they were here.
+ * - deq 18 and 20..22 are the DSL, checksum and directpath ports, which are
+ * not Ethernet and have no netdev.
  */
 static const struct {
 	u32 deq_port;
@@ -339,18 +354,10 @@ static const struct {
 	u32 dma_ctrl;
 	u32 dma_chan;
 } cbm_dqm_dma_eth_rows[] = {
-	{  6, 16, 2,  1 },
 	{  7, 17, 2,  2 },
 	{  8, 18, 2,  3 },
 	{  9, 19, 2,  4 },
 	{ 10, 20, 2,  5 },
-	{ 11, 21, 2,  6 },
-	{ 12, 22, 2,  9 },
-	{ 13, 23, 2, 10 },
-	{ 14, 24, 2, 11 },
-	{ 15, 25, 2, 12 },
-	{ 16, 26, 2, 13 },
-	{ 17, 27, 2, 14 },
 	{ 19, 28, 1, 15 },
 };
 
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/datapath/datapath_misc.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/datapath/datapath_misc.c
index 5a26750292d1..dbca4c609601 100644
